So, not their holiday.

148
00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:14.950
No, it's not.

149
00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:16.570
I loved that.

150
00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:17.950
All right.

151
00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:19.610
Fast fact number three, everybody.

152
00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:21.150
You've been waiting for it.

153
00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:23.270
You've been wondering when it's going to happen.

154
00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:26.690
Then, here is your landline update.

155
00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:30.750
Okay, let's go.

156
00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:38.490
On March 16th, on the front page of my Sunday New York Times, which you know I get every week,

157
00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:41.210
there was an article with this headline.

158
00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:43.590
I'd never been so excited.

159
00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:46.190
It said this, quote,

160
00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:51.750
Landline users remain proudly old-fashioned in the digital age.

161
00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:55.710
Gosh, this is tying back into our opening of this whole episode.

162
00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:58.230
This is what old people talk about.

163
00:06:58.470 --> 00:06:59.170
It's true.

164
00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:05.250
I did notice that most of the people quoted in the article were over 60.

165
00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:10.750
So, the reason for this article is because there are phone companies that want to phase out the landline.

166
00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:17.570
The number of copper landlines, which is also known as plain old telephone service, or POTS.

167
00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:18.750
POTS?

168
00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:19.390
POTS.

169
00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:19.850
Okay.

170
00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:23.230
If you have POTS, you have plain old telephone service.

171
00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:30.570
The number of people who have this fell by 89% over the last 20 years in California.

172
00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:31.430
Wow.

173
00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:32.250
I know.

174
00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:36.150
So, here's the thing that I want to get out there to everyone.

175
00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:37.010
Okay?

176
00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:37.520
Okay.

177
00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:38.750
This is important to me.

178
00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:39.630
All right.

179
00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.290
It's like POTS to me.

180
00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:43.430
POTS is like POTS to you?

181
00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:43.890
Yes.

182
00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:44.390
Okay.

183
00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.950
A landline is not just a home phone.

184
00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:52.290
A true landline has to be hooked up to a copper wire.

185
00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:55.130
And most phone companies are phasing these out.

186
00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:57.910
And instead, you can have a landline.

187
00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:01.130
You can have a phone that goes into the wall.

188
00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:04.630
But they are putting them on fiber optic cable.

189
00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:06.410
And it's not a landline.

190
00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:07.930
Not a true landline.

191
00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:15.370
And the reason they're doing this is because those fiber optic cables can also carry cable and internet services.

192
00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:20.190
So, it's like they can hook everything up to that as it comes into your house.

193
00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:23.650
And this is what my phone company did to me without telling me.

194
00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:29.090
They switched my landline, quote unquote, to a fiber optic cable line.

195
00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:32.070
And I had to call and make them put me back on a copper line.

196
00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:36.570
Because power failures can take out fiber optic cables.

197
00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:38.870
They depend on electricity to work.

198
00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:42.510
So, this is why your Wi-Fi goes out when your power goes out.

199
00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.130
But copper wires are self-powered.

200
00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:48.390
And they do not go down in the event of a power outage.

201
00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:53.810
I will say, I loved everyone who was quoted in this article.

202
00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:57.210
They are sassy, they are stubborn, and they are my people.

203
00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:02.290
Derek Shaw, 68, of York, Pennsylvania, loves his landline.

204
00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:05.590
He also prefers speaking to people face-to-face.

205
00:09:05.590 --> 00:09:06.790
He's not a fan of Zoom.

206
00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:08.390
And he listens to records.

207
00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:09.250
Okay?

208
00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:12.910
He said, quote, I've never even thought about giving up my landline.

209
00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:15.050
And I'll go kicking and screaming when I have to.

210
00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:16.630
That's right.

211
00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:23.630
Here are some reasons, not related to a zombie apocalypse, where you might want a landline.

212
00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:28.910
I know I often talk about the zombie apocalypse as my reason for needing these services.

213
00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:30.770
But there's other reasons.

214
00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:31.250
Okay.

215
00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:35.450
If there's a power outage from a flood, a fire, or other natural disaster.

216
00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:38.070
Katie Lanza, who was 37.

217
00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:39.570
She's from 37.

218
00:09:39.590 --> 00:09:41.110
We got a young'un in there.

219
00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:41.510
Yeah.

220
00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:45.810
She's from Fort Worth, and she said that her dog chewed her cell phone.

221
00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:52.610
And while she was waiting to get her replacement, she got sick in the middle of the night, and she needed help, and she didn't have a phone.

222
00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:54.730
She had to go to her neighbor's house to use a phone.

223
00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:57.570
And ever since, she's had a true landline.

224
00:09:57.970 --> 00:09:58.850
So there you go.

225
00:09:58.970 --> 00:09:59.290
Mm-hmm.

226
00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:01.390
Flood, fire, natural disaster.

227
00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:03.270
Something happens to your cell phone.

228
00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:04.610
I'm not done.

229
00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:05.190
Okay?

230
00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:08.190
I didn't think you were, because you're holding several pieces of paper.

231
00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:08.650
Yeah.

232
00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:18.050
Francella Jackson, 61, from Illinois, said she worries about cyber attacks disrupting her cell service, and that's why she has a landline.

233
00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:27.610
After I read this article, it compelled me to double-check with my phone company to make sure that they hadn't switched me again, and I do still have a POTS.

234
00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:29.610
I do have that copper landline.

235
00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:30.330
Good for you.

236
00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:34.350
And we have the cutest little old-timey phone with the little buttons.

237
00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:36.850
My kids are mesmerized by it.

238
00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:44.230
They love to call us on our cell phones from the landline and, like, prank us.

239
00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:45.370
That's cute.

240
00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:46.050
They love it.

241
00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:47.950
Push-button phone, old-timey.

242
00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:50.170
There you have it, everyone.

243
00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:52.490
Public service announcement.

244
00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:54.370
You might want a POTS.

245
00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:55.790
I think it's a great idea.

246
00:10:55.930 --> 00:10:59.710
I'm also very curious what they wrapped the fiber optics cables in.

247
00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:00.170
Mm-hmm.

248
00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:03.550
Because, as you know, my car wasn't working.

249
00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.490
All the computer buttons were flashing.

250
00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:07.490
Seatbelt alert.

251
00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:08.770
Drive train malfunction.

252
00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:09.570
Airbag.

253
00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:10.450
I'm like, what's happening?

254
00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:16.270
Well, I have, as it turns out, massive rodent damage.

