00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:49.180
Is that the coolest thing?

772
00:32:49.360 --> 00:32:50.460
It's the coolest thing.

773
00:32:50.600 --> 00:32:51.860
It meant so much to us.

774
00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:57.060
Well, in this email chain, mine was scheduled for December 19th at 5 p.m.

775
00:32:57.360 --> 00:32:57.680
Wow.

776
00:32:58.000 --> 00:32:58.220
Mm-hmm.

777
00:32:58.500 --> 00:32:59.740
Do you remember doing it?

778
00:32:59.860 --> 00:33:00.560
I do, actually.

779
00:33:00.700 --> 00:33:05.780
I remember them asking me what were my hopes and dreams for my character.

780
00:33:06.440 --> 00:33:10.800
If there was one last thing that she could do, where I thought she would sort of end the

781
00:33:10.800 --> 00:33:12.960
series at in her story arc.

782
00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:13.300
Mm-hmm.

783
00:33:13.440 --> 00:33:16.860
And I told them I thought that Angela should end up with Dwight.

784
00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:18.560
Yes, 100%.

785
00:33:18.560 --> 00:33:21.020
And I hope that that relationship reconciled itself.

786
00:33:21.460 --> 00:33:21.780
Amazing.

787
00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:29.040
Third email was to you, subject title, OMG, all caps, exclamation point.

788
00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:30.380
Ooh, leaning in.

789
00:33:30.580 --> 00:33:30.860
Mm-hmm.

790
00:33:31.220 --> 00:33:33.280
Then when you open up the email, it said,

791
00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:38.580
Lady, I am going to co-host Carson Daly's New Year's Eve show.

792
00:33:38.840 --> 00:33:40.100
Oh, my gosh.

793
00:33:40.100 --> 00:33:41.860
I remember when you did that.

794
00:33:42.060 --> 00:33:45.100
You wrote back, OMG, exclamation point.

795
00:33:45.220 --> 00:33:47.280
That is amazing, exclamation point.

796
00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:49.380
Will you be outside the whole time?

797
00:33:49.520 --> 00:33:50.060
Question mark.

798
00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:52.400
Tell me everything at lunch, exclamation point.

799
00:33:52.720 --> 00:33:53.760
Here's the thing.

800
00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:57.320
Even when we were younger, I was still worried about the weather.

801
00:33:57.540 --> 00:33:58.480
It's cold.

802
00:33:58.480 --> 00:33:59.420
It is.

803
00:33:59.540 --> 00:34:01.240
And I was always cold back then.

804
00:34:01.400 --> 00:34:04.780
So that was a legitimate, like, immediate concern for me as well.

805
00:34:04.920 --> 00:34:09.500
I just know every year that I watch those guys outside, I wonder how cold they are.

806
00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:12.280
I think about them at the Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade.

807
00:34:12.280 --> 00:34:13.960
I think about them on New Year's Eve.

808
00:34:14.160 --> 00:34:19.200
I was so excited to get some insider info on how cold it actually is doing that.

809
00:34:19.360 --> 00:34:20.320
Angela, tell everyone.

810
00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:22.180
Well, here's the thing.

811
00:34:22.740 --> 00:34:28.080
Anyone you see that's on a platform, like, you know how, like, you have the New Year's

812
00:34:28.080 --> 00:34:30.040
Eve show and they're on the main platform?

813
00:34:30.240 --> 00:34:30.400
Yes.

814
00:34:30.560 --> 00:34:33.460
And then there's people kind of out in the street and on the ground and around.

815
00:34:33.680 --> 00:34:35.540
The people on the platform have it beSt.

816
00:34:35.580 --> 00:34:41.120
It's still cold, but they have these tubes, like, by their feet that you can't see.

817
00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:41.940
It's off camera.

818
00:34:42.100 --> 00:34:43.080
That pump in heat.

819
00:34:43.760 --> 00:34:44.240
All right?

820
00:34:44.320 --> 00:34:47.760
So, yeah, your face is getting a little cold, but your bottom half is at least warm.

821
00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:52.260
But anyone you see that's doing the man-on-the-street bits, they're freezing their ass off.

822
00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:53.480
That was me.

823
00:34:53.980 --> 00:34:56.480
So, the whole night, I'm doing man-on-the-street bits.

824
00:34:56.560 --> 00:35:00.560
There was one point where, like, I remember I was like, I'm not sure if my words sound

825
00:35:00.560 --> 00:35:03.980
correct because I can't really move my lips because I was so cold.

826
00:35:03.980 --> 00:35:04.420
You're so cold.

827
00:35:04.640 --> 00:35:09.140
So, at the end of the night, I joined Carson Daly on the platform, and I was like,

828
00:35:09.320 --> 00:35:10.760
son of a bitch.

829
00:35:11.040 --> 00:35:13.220
It is so warm up here.

830
00:35:13.700 --> 00:35:14.820
What the hell?

831
00:35:14.820 --> 00:35:18.780
I had no idea that they had tubes of heat on the platform.

832
00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:20.100
Okay.

833
00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:24.780
Lastly, the whole entire cast got an email from the production office titled,

834
00:35:25.620 --> 00:35:26.100
Sinapalooza.

835
00:35:26.400 --> 00:35:28.940
Oh, we had a Sinapalooza before we left?

836
00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:29.320
Yep.

837
00:35:29.420 --> 00:35:29.900
And I looked.

838
00:35:29.980 --> 00:35:31.280
It was also in the call sheet.

839
00:35:31.340 --> 00:35:31.560
Yep.

840
00:35:31.940 --> 00:35:34.680
Starting on Tuesday, we'll be tackling Sinapalooza.

841
00:35:34.880 --> 00:35:35.360
Get ready.

842
00:35:35.840 --> 00:35:36.460
All right.

843
00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:38.380
Here's what Sinapalooza was, everyone.

844
00:35:38.380 --> 00:35:45.220
They would take our lunch tables back in the back, and about half of them would be covered

845
00:35:45.220 --> 00:35:48.400
with scripts, posters.

846
00:35:49.340 --> 00:35:50.380
DVD boxes.

847
00:35:50.800 --> 00:35:51.060
Yes.

848
00:35:51.260 --> 00:35:55.740
And there would be Sharpies out, and you had to go down the table, and you had to sign

849
00:35:55.740 --> 00:35:56.620
every item.

850
00:35:56.920 --> 00:36:01.200
I need to put the size of the table in context and why it was called Sinapalooza.

851
00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:04.260
This lunch area fed 300 people.

852
00:36:04.560 --> 00:36:11.320
So this lunch table, it was actually three tables that were like half the size of the

853
00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:12.140
soundstage.

854
00:36:12.300 --> 00:36:12.680
Yeah.

855
00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:15.740
And there would be items all the way down it.

856
00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:19.840
And you would start at one end, and you would make it as far as you could make it between

857
00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:23.320
a scene, and then they would mark where you made it to.

858
00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:26.520
Okay, Angela made it through the first half of posters.

859
00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:30.100
So the next time, she's got to start right here.

860
00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:30.640
Yes.

861
00:36:30.640 --> 00:36:32.840
And you would tackle it, and it took a week.

862
00:36:33.020 --> 00:36:36.360
It would take all week, because you could only do it in starts and stops.

863
00:36:36.920 --> 00:36:38.660
Oh my gosh, Sinapalooza.

864
00:36:39.360 --> 00:36:40.120
Sinapalooza, yeah.

865
00:36:40.680 --> 00:36:43.180
And that's everything from my digital clutter for this week.

866
00:36:43.280 --> 00:36:45.360
That was a treasure trove.

867
00:36:45.440 --> 00:36:46.000
My goodness.

868
00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:51.100
That was really a trip down memory lane, and I think gave a lot of insight into some of the

869
00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:52.480
stuff that happened behind the scenes.

870
00:36:52.660 --> 00:36:53.380
Thank you, Angela.

871
00:36:54.500 --> 00:36:57.780
Well, now Pam and Jim are going to arrive to their restaurant.

872
00:36:57.780 --> 00:37:00.540
Brian is sitting at a table for four.

873
00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:02.940
He has an empty seat next to him.

874
00:37:03.500 --> 00:37:03.780
Yep.

875
00:37:04.180 --> 00:37:05.840
They ask, where is Alyssa?

876
00:37:06.100 --> 00:37:10.700
And that is when Brian says, oh, yeah, we're splitting up.

877
00:37:10.700 --> 00:37:12.720
I do have a bulls**ter to play.

878
00:37:12.860 --> 00:37:13.520
What is it?

879
00:37:13.880 --> 00:37:14.440
Lady?

880
00:37:14.960 --> 00:37:15.180
What?

881
00:37:15.660 --> 00:37:19.920
I call bulls**t on Pam inviting Brian and Alyssa to Valentine's lunch.

882
00:37:20.860 --> 00:37:21.420
Why?

883
00:37:23.360 --> 00:37:26.620
Because, like, her husband is working in another city.

884
00:37:27.180 --> 00:37:29.420
This is so special that they got to be together.

885
00:37:29.980 --> 00:37:32.080
Don't have Brian and Alyssa at lunch.

886
00:37:32.440 --> 00:37:39.880
What if it happened this way, where Brian reached out and was like, we'd love to take

887
00:37:39.880 --> 00:37:40.820
you guys to lunch.

888
00:37:41.000 --> 00:37:43.200
Like, you know, it sounds like they used to get together.

889
00:37:43.620 --> 00:37:44.120
Yeah.

890
00:37:44.300 --> 00:37:47.140
Remember, he was going to be in Jim's wedding party.

891
00:37:47.480 --> 00:37:49.040
Jim's not around a lot.

892
00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:53.300
But would you want to go to lunch on this one day that Jim is in town?

893
00:37:53.440 --> 00:37:54.640
Let's do a couple's lunch.

894
00:37:55.020 --> 00:37:59.740
And then the idea was maybe Jim and Pam were going to have a romantic evening together, even

895
00:37:59.740 --> 00:38:03.120
though they seem to have no plans for that, because they're terrible at Valentine's Day.

896
00:38:05.060 --> 00:38:06.800
I mean, I guess.

897
00:38:06.960 --> 00:38:08.500
But I feel like if you get—

898
00:38:08.500 --> 00:38:09.900
So it's not like Pam reached out.

899
00:38:09.980 --> 00:38:11.240
It's like he reached out?

900
00:38:11.240 --> 00:38:11.760
Okay, fine.

901
00:38:12.040 --> 00:38:13.920
If he reached out and then I guess she felt bad.

902
00:38:14.020 --> 00:38:15.780
But still, yes.

903
00:38:15.960 --> 00:38:16.220
Okay.

904
00:38:16.220 --> 00:38:20.540
But here's the bullshit card that I have to play, although it tracks with how Jim and

905
00:38:20.540 --> 00:38:26.380
Pam are communicating lately, which is if I got a text from someone or if I had this

906
00:38:26.380 --> 00:38:28.940
idea, I would speak to my partner before committing.

907
00:38:29.220 --> 00:38:29.460
Right.

908
00:38:29.620 --> 00:38:33.440
You would not commit to plans without being like, hey, I know you're only here for one

909
00:38:33.440 --> 00:38:34.280
day this week.

910
00:38:34.640 --> 00:38:35.040
Yeah.

911
00:38:35.180 --> 00:38:35.840
It's Valentine's Day.

912
00:38:35.980 --> 00:38:37.660
You moved things around so you could be here.

913
00:38:38.180 --> 00:38:39.260
Brian reached out.

914
00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:40.580
Him and Alyssa want to have lunch.

915
00:38:40.780 --> 00:38:43.080
And then Jim would have probably been like, oh, no, let's not.

916
00:38:43.280 --> 00:38:44.860
Let's just have the day for us.

917
00:38:45.120 --> 00:38:45.280
Yeah.

918
00:38:45.280 --> 00:38:49.480
If we get a break at lunch, let's go home and have sex because the kids are at daycare.

919
00:38:49.860 --> 00:38:50.420
Uh-huh.

920
00:38:50.660 --> 00:38:53.700
And you know we're not going to tonight because we're going to be too tired.

921
00:38:56.000 --> 00:38:58.440
That's a very specific example.

922
00:38:59.660 --> 00:39:00.540
Very specific.

923
00:39:03.860 --> 00:39:10.440
Well, listen, if you want to know this restaurant location, it was Marston's on Newhall Ranch

924
00:39:10.440 --> 00:39:10.820
Road.

925
00:39:10.820 --> 00:39:11.940
It's still there.

926
00:39:12.520 --> 00:39:17.700
And it was recognized by Zagat as the number one place for breakfast in L.A.

927
00:39:17.820 --> 00:39:18.680
and it was

928
00:39:18.680 --> 00:39:21.800
named the best breakfast in California by the Food Network magazine.

929
00:39:22.280 --> 00:39:23.880
I've always called it Zagat.

930
00:39:24.060 --> 00:39:24.940
It's Zagat?

931
00:39:25.060 --> 00:39:26.020
It's Zagat.

932
00:39:26.120 --> 00:39:27.040
I never knew that.

933
00:39:27.220 --> 00:39:30.080
It is hard for me to say it correctly because I thought it was Zagat.

934
00:39:30.420 --> 00:39:30.720
Yeah.

935
00:39:30.720 --> 00:39:32.260
And we called it Zagat forever.

936
00:39:32.420 --> 00:39:32.580
Yeah.

937
00:39:32.720 --> 00:39:34.260
But it's apparently Zagat.

938
00:39:34.440 --> 00:39:35.000
Zagat.

939
00:39:35.220 --> 00:39:41.720
Dwight is back in Andy's office signing off on more paperwork as, quote, fake Andy when

940
00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:44.160
the real Andy appears behind him.

941
00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:47.140
I have a call sheet tidbit for this scene, Angela.

942
00:39:47.380 --> 00:39:48.040
Let's hear it.

943
00:39:48.200 --> 00:39:54.880
We shot this scene on Monday, and the back of every call sheet has a section called Daily

944
00:39:54.880 --> 00:39:55.440
Notes.

945
00:39:56.360 --> 00:40:00.760
This is a little spot on the call sheet that includes notes for costumes, hair, makeup,

946
00:40:00.920 --> 00:40:01.640
props, sound.

947
00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:06.660
And under props in the Daily Notes for this scene, it said this.

948
00:40:07.280 --> 00:40:08.080
Props note.

949
00:40:08.920 --> 00:40:15.320
But Andy's satchel, Andy's stacked up mail, sales order to sign, pen, and then in parentheses,

950
00:40:15.740 --> 00:40:16.060
multiples.

951
00:40:17.160 --> 00:40:19.960
And I was like, what happens to the pen?

952
00:40:20.180 --> 00:40:21.380
Why do they need multiples?

953
00:40:21.680 --> 00:40:22.420
I'll tell you why.

954
00:40:22.940 --> 00:40:24.340
Because Rainn Wilson was using it.

955
00:40:24.420 --> 00:40:24.820
That's right.

956
00:40:24.920 --> 00:40:26.480
Rainn Wilson loved options.

957
00:40:26.720 --> 00:40:27.960
He liked lots of choices.

958
00:40:27.960 --> 00:40:32.340
This is why I called him Carrot Top, because he always needed his box of different tricks.

959
00:40:32.740 --> 00:40:36.860
I have no doubt that Rainn wanted to pick the pen that Dwight would be using.

960
00:40:36.860 --> 00:40:41.760
I wondered if it was because perhaps he might end up throwing it or doing something with

961
00:40:41.760 --> 00:40:42.880
it, and they had to have more.

962
00:40:43.220 --> 00:40:47.020
No, I think it just meant that Rainn either wanted to click it or twist it.

963
00:40:47.560 --> 00:40:48.060
Right.

964
00:40:48.260 --> 00:40:48.480
Yes.

965
00:40:48.760 --> 00:40:48.880
Something.

966
00:40:49.200 --> 00:40:49.640
Multiples.

967
00:40:50.120 --> 00:40:52.040
Well, the hair and makeup note was this.

968
00:40:52.400 --> 00:40:59.300
Andy is tanned, unkempt, as scripted, scraggly beard, unkempt hair, long, dirty fingernails.

969
00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:06.780
And the costume note was, Andy is unkempt, as scripted, a Gaia Berra shirt, Sansa belt,

970
00:41:07.200 --> 00:41:08.900
slacks, and woven loafers.

971
00:41:09.040 --> 00:41:10.360
I love Sansa belt.

972
00:41:10.520 --> 00:41:11.700
It's one of my favorite terMs.

973
00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:12.920
What is it?

974
00:41:13.460 --> 00:41:14.640
Sans a belt.

975
00:41:14.900 --> 00:41:15.560
No belt.

976
00:41:15.760 --> 00:41:16.220
Oh!

977
00:41:16.400 --> 00:41:17.040
Sans a belt.

978
00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:18.640
I thought it was a kind of belt.

979
00:41:20.080 --> 00:41:23.660
My friend's dad used to always wear pants that were Sansa belt.

980
00:41:23.840 --> 00:41:28.920
He's a very funny comedian, and he did an improv monologue one time about—his name is

981
00:41:28.920 --> 00:41:29.500
Mike Bunan.

982
00:41:29.560 --> 00:41:33.280
He did an improv monologue one time about his dad's Sansa belt pants.

983
00:41:33.540 --> 00:41:34.800
I'll never forget it.

984
00:41:35.460 --> 00:41:41.300
Well, the Dunder Mifflin crew now return from their mini-mall excursion to find Andy waiting

985
00:41:41.300 --> 00:41:41.760
for them.

986
00:41:41.940 --> 00:41:46.280
Andy has so many classic, like, pun phrases in this episode.

987
00:41:46.420 --> 00:41:46.940
Oh, my gosh.

988
00:41:46.940 --> 00:41:47.740
Here's a good one.

989
00:41:48.100 --> 00:41:51.800
Cancel my order from Zappos because the loafers have arrived.

990
00:41:52.700 --> 00:41:53.280
Mm-hmm.

991
00:41:53.540 --> 00:41:57.720
I mean, who does he think he is calling anyone a loafer?

992
00:41:58.220 --> 00:41:59.120
I know.

993
00:41:59.700 --> 00:42:02.340
Oscar says, hey, we thought you were coming back tomorrow.

994
00:42:02.820 --> 00:42:05.320
And then Andy says, no, I came back early.

995
00:42:05.500 --> 00:42:08.240
It's a romantic Valentine's Day surprise for Aaron.

996
00:42:08.480 --> 00:42:12.400
I did think it was really funny when he tried to run up to hug her, and she's trying to give

997
00:42:12.400 --> 00:42:13.280
him just a high five.

998
00:42:15.160 --> 00:42:19.640
This is when Dwight says, hey, maybe you should put on a tie because David Wallace is going

999
00:42:19.640 --> 00:42:20.460
to be here in an hour.

1000
00:42:20.620 --> 00:42:23.260
And Andy's like, oh, yeah, that's actually why I came back.

1001
00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:26.080
I mean, and also to surprise Aaron.

1002
00:42:26.600 --> 00:42:26.860
Oh, Andy.

1003
00:42:27.440 --> 00:42:27.920
Ugh.

1004
00:42:28.740 --> 00:42:34.020
This is when Clark is like, why are you looking forward to this meeting with David Wallace?

1005
00:42:34.400 --> 00:42:38.460
Isn't Wallace just going to, like, yell at you because you've been gone for three months?

1006
00:42:38.980 --> 00:42:42.840
And then everyone's like, wait, David Wallace knew you were gone, right?

1007
00:42:43.020 --> 00:42:46.380
And this is when Andy shares that actually he kept in touch with David Wallace.

1008
00:42:46.500 --> 00:42:47.760
He emailed him all the time.

1009
00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:51.020
He would get high-speed internet at this cafe.

1010
00:42:51.640 --> 00:42:55.400
Aaron has a talking head where she says he only emailed me four times.

1011
00:42:56.580 --> 00:42:56.760
Yeah.

1012
00:42:57.100 --> 00:42:58.080
That stings.

1013
00:42:58.420 --> 00:43:00.580
Andy notices that Pam and Jim are gone.

1014
00:43:00.740 --> 00:43:02.640
He has another snark remark about it.

1015
00:43:02.640 --> 00:43:07.240
And Oscar is like, I find it curious that you're concerned about people's absence from work.

1016
00:43:07.420 --> 00:43:09.220
This is a great Oscar snark.

1017
00:43:09.420 --> 00:43:10.120
Thank you, Oscar.

1018
00:43:10.600 --> 00:43:15.260
Back at the restaurant, Brian is now sharing kind of about how him and Alyssa just drifted apart.

1019
00:43:15.460 --> 00:43:21.220
And he said something I thought was great, which is he said it's when they stopped fighting that he knew it was over.

1020
00:43:21.720 --> 00:43:21.900
Yeah.

1021
00:43:22.240 --> 00:43:24.140
And he gets really emotional.

