1262
00:54:33.760 --> 00:54:37.080
And he says, you know, the guy that lit the whole warehouse on fire.

1263
00:54:37.940 --> 00:54:39.880
Everyone else catches on pretty quickly.

1264
00:54:40.040 --> 00:54:42.540
Phyllis says, oh, yeah, and then we started selling balloons.

1265
00:54:42.960 --> 00:54:48.380
And then Clark chimes in and says, yeah, and Kathy Ireland is going to be our official spokesperson.

1266
00:54:49.300 --> 00:54:51.540
Yeah, spokes babe for Dunder Mifflin.

1267
00:54:51.640 --> 00:54:53.940
She's going to be topless on the European billboard.

1268
00:54:55.080 --> 00:54:59.820
Everyone set your DVRs for 16 minutes and 35 seconds.

1269
00:55:00.140 --> 00:55:04.920
Angela, you are very upset that Kathy Ireland will be topless on the European billboards.

1270
00:55:05.100 --> 00:55:08.000
The look, you are horrified.

1271
00:55:08.320 --> 00:55:10.120
You can't believe this news.

1272
00:55:10.380 --> 00:55:11.520
It's not in the script.

1273
00:55:11.680 --> 00:55:13.680
I think I just chose to react that way.

1274
00:55:13.880 --> 00:55:15.080
So funny.

1275
00:55:15.080 --> 00:55:19.740
You know, we also got a very, very funny fan catch from Alanya M.

1276
00:55:19.840 --> 00:55:21.900
in South Carolina who said,

1277
00:55:22.100 --> 00:55:24.280
not a question, but a wardrobe catch.

1278
00:55:24.460 --> 00:55:28.940
Did you all notice that Kevin, Dwight, and David Wallace are all dressed almost identically?

1279
00:55:29.280 --> 00:55:33.200
Their characters are so different, but they're all wearing a greenish blue shirt.

1280
00:55:33.200 --> 00:55:35.340
And it fits them all so well.

1281
00:55:35.820 --> 00:55:36.520
You know what?

1282
00:55:36.520 --> 00:55:39.540
I did not notice this, but I went back to look.

1283
00:55:39.780 --> 00:55:40.880
I took a photo.

1284
00:55:41.140 --> 00:55:42.240
It's very nice.

1285
00:55:42.560 --> 00:55:44.040
I completely agree.

1286
00:55:44.040 --> 00:55:45.500
It's very nice.

1287
00:55:45.880 --> 00:55:46.280
It is.

1288
00:55:46.400 --> 00:55:49.360
The color is lovely and it fits them all really well.

1289
00:55:49.680 --> 00:55:57.580
And they are all wearing like this icy blue undershirt and then this sort of like dark gray suit.

1290
00:55:57.920 --> 00:55:59.180
Oh, yeah, they are.

1291
00:55:59.820 --> 00:56:01.420
Kevin, Dwight, and David Wallace.

1292
00:56:01.700 --> 00:56:02.560
It's really lovely.

1293
00:56:02.700 --> 00:56:03.520
You can put it in stories.

1294
00:56:03.760 --> 00:56:04.200
Okay.

1295
00:56:04.480 --> 00:56:09.340
Well, now we go to Andy's office because he's been totally set up by the Dunder Mifflin crew.

1296
00:56:09.620 --> 00:56:12.580
And Wallace is talking to Andy and Andy says,

1297
00:56:12.580 --> 00:56:17.080
Hey, it's so great about that balloon game that we got going on, especially in Wake of the Fire.

1298
00:56:17.460 --> 00:56:19.580
Wallace is like, what are you talking about?

1299
00:56:20.160 --> 00:56:21.740
There's no fire in the warehouse.

1300
00:56:22.100 --> 00:56:22.380
I know.

1301
00:56:22.460 --> 00:56:25.180
And then Andy's like, oh, I mean, metaphorically.

1302
00:56:25.340 --> 00:56:27.280
And he figures out what they were trying to do.

1303
00:56:28.180 --> 00:56:33.040
Then later, Aaron's going to go into Andy's office and say, I don't love you anymore.

1304
00:56:33.040 --> 00:56:34.180
You were gone a long time.

1305
00:56:34.300 --> 00:56:35.820
You didn't email me very much.

1306
00:56:36.020 --> 00:56:38.300
To be fair, you retweeted me quite a bit.

1307
00:56:38.300 --> 00:56:40.340
What is Aaron tweeting about?

1308
00:56:40.540 --> 00:56:41.240
I don't know.

1309
00:56:41.480 --> 00:56:42.360
Wouldn't you love to know?

1310
00:56:42.520 --> 00:56:42.800
Yeah.

1311
00:56:43.360 --> 00:56:45.760
We had fake Twitter accounts, remember?

1312
00:56:46.020 --> 00:56:46.960
For a few characters.

1313
00:56:47.840 --> 00:56:50.220
Andy insists there's a lot of love between them.

1314
00:56:50.380 --> 00:56:52.580
Aaron says, there isn't any love on my side.

1315
00:56:52.580 --> 00:56:54.640
And he says, you can just fake it.

1316
00:56:55.140 --> 00:56:57.380
He's not letting her break up with him.

1317
00:56:57.500 --> 00:56:57.720
Nope.

1318
00:56:58.220 --> 00:57:00.220
Well, now we've got a pretty big scene.

1319
00:57:01.180 --> 00:57:04.780
Jim is going to tell Pam they should cancel that bottle of wine.

1320
00:57:04.780 --> 00:57:06.960
And she should just drop them at the bus station.

1321
00:57:06.960 --> 00:57:07.580
Yeah.

1322
00:57:07.920 --> 00:57:08.360
I'm sorry.

1323
00:57:08.620 --> 00:57:11.260
Do you not want to see your f***ing children, Jim?

1324
00:57:11.480 --> 00:57:12.180
Thank you.

1325
00:57:12.400 --> 00:57:14.500
You don't want to just, like, say hi to your kids?

1326
00:57:14.700 --> 00:57:15.160
I know.

1327
00:57:15.160 --> 00:57:17.300
You don't want to go home and be a dad tonight?

1328
00:57:17.540 --> 00:57:18.380
I mean, yeah.

1329
00:57:18.480 --> 00:57:18.960
Okay, fine.

1330
00:57:18.960 --> 00:57:20.440
You and your wife are having a fight.

1331
00:57:20.640 --> 00:57:22.100
At least go hang with your kids.

1332
00:57:22.900 --> 00:57:23.300
Sorry.

1333
00:57:23.560 --> 00:57:24.780
Had to do that rant.

1334
00:57:25.260 --> 00:57:27.020
Does Jim remember he has children?

1335
00:57:27.280 --> 00:57:28.060
I don't know.

1336
00:57:30.060 --> 00:57:35.960
I do feel like we needed to have more scenes of him just calling and, like, talking to the kids or something.

1337
00:57:35.960 --> 00:57:40.840
The only time he does it is for Cece's dance recital when they're driving in the car.

1338
00:57:42.020 --> 00:57:46.020
He says he has work to do in Philly, and she's probably got things to do as well.

1339
00:57:46.100 --> 00:57:47.700
And they'll probably just end up fighting anyway.

1340
00:57:48.120 --> 00:57:50.040
And then he's like, oh, and here's a gift.

1341
00:57:50.040 --> 00:57:51.500
I didn't have time to wrap it.

1342
00:57:51.500 --> 00:57:55.220
But, oh, I am so sorry, but I am going to toot my own horn.

1343
00:57:55.420 --> 00:57:55.800
Okay.

1344
00:57:56.080 --> 00:57:58.100
I love my reaction to the present.

1345
00:57:58.540 --> 00:57:59.480
I love it.

1346
00:57:59.680 --> 00:58:02.480
When I'm like, oh, you kept it.

1347
00:58:02.660 --> 00:58:03.140
Thanks.

1348
00:58:03.920 --> 00:58:10.080
Well, it's funny that you mention that because there was a note in the shooting draft about your reaction.

1349
00:58:10.340 --> 00:58:11.140
What did it say?

1350
00:58:11.360 --> 00:58:15.400
The whole scene was actually longer, and some of it's in deleted scenes.

1351
00:58:15.880 --> 00:58:18.980
This is another thing I'm glad they cut out.

1352
00:58:19.160 --> 00:58:19.980
Do you want to hear it?

1353
00:58:19.980 --> 00:58:20.420
Yes.

1354
00:58:20.920 --> 00:58:21.240
Okay.

1355
00:58:21.580 --> 00:58:22.880
Pam has just gotten the gift.

1356
00:58:23.380 --> 00:58:25.560
The shooting draft says, Pam opens it.

1357
00:58:25.680 --> 00:58:27.100
It's one of her drawings framed.

1358
00:58:27.420 --> 00:58:30.920
It doesn't mean what it once would have, but she's still appreciative.

1359
00:58:31.340 --> 00:58:32.240
That was my note.

1360
00:58:32.380 --> 00:58:33.400
That was my acting note.

1361
00:58:33.560 --> 00:58:35.500
Pam says, I didn't know you kept this.

1362
00:58:35.620 --> 00:58:35.980
Thank you.

1363
00:58:36.340 --> 00:58:37.420
Jim says, no problem.

1364
00:58:37.820 --> 00:58:39.060
Pam looks at Jim.

1365
00:58:39.460 --> 00:58:40.840
He avoids her gaze.

1366
00:58:41.260 --> 00:58:43.400
Then it cuts to a Pam talking head.

1367
00:58:43.840 --> 00:58:48.320
Pam says, my husband Jim is very special, and he's stubborn.

1368
00:58:48.320 --> 00:58:50.240
And he's making me insane.

1369
00:58:50.460 --> 00:58:51.680
And he's impossible.

1370
00:58:51.980 --> 00:58:54.240
And I will not let him give up on me.

1371
00:58:54.740 --> 00:58:55.080
Oh.

1372
00:58:55.380 --> 00:58:59.580
And then it cuts back to the scene where she says, I think you should stay and fight.

1373
00:59:00.080 --> 00:59:00.340
Hmm.

1374
00:59:00.960 --> 00:59:01.420
What?

1375
00:59:01.700 --> 00:59:04.040
Pam won't let him give up on her?

1376
00:59:05.300 --> 00:59:06.420
Excuse me?

1377
00:59:07.560 --> 00:59:08.800
What the hell?

1378
00:59:09.700 --> 00:59:14.060
You know, a lot of people wrote in to comment on this drawing, by the way.

1379
00:59:14.260 --> 00:59:16.580
I'm going to read this one from Sarah S.

1380
00:59:16.700 --> 00:59:17.780
in Montgomery, Alabama.

1381
00:59:18.100 --> 00:59:21.900
Some people were confused about what is this drawing.

1382
00:59:22.520 --> 00:59:25.820
Well, Sarah said, I've been waiting forever for this.

1383
00:59:25.820 --> 00:59:33.020
The gift that Jim gives Pam is a sketch from her sketchbook from when she returned from art school.

1384
00:59:33.280 --> 00:59:36.020
And Jim had looked through the sketchbook and loved it.

1385
00:59:36.100 --> 00:59:38.180
But it is a deleted scene.

1386
00:59:38.640 --> 00:59:39.000
Hmm.

1387
00:59:39.340 --> 00:59:44.160
So Sarah says, this is the proof that deleted scenes are canon.

1388
00:59:44.600 --> 00:59:46.280
That they do live in the show.

1389
00:59:46.560 --> 00:59:47.900
Sarah, wow.

1390
00:59:48.440 --> 00:59:50.240
That kind of blows my mind.

1391
00:59:50.400 --> 00:59:50.760
Mm-hmm.

1392
00:59:50.760 --> 00:59:53.320
We also got this letter from Brittany T.

1393
00:59:53.440 --> 00:59:56.580
in Dallas, Texas, who said, I'm getting married in 2024,

1394
00:59:56.580 --> 01:00:01.920
and my fiancé and I have talked a lot about Jim and Pam's relationship throughout the show,

1395
01:00:02.200 --> 01:00:04.040
specifically in season nine.

1396
01:00:04.760 --> 01:00:08.580
Our many debates and conversations led to the same conclusion.

1397
01:00:09.320 --> 01:00:14.800
I feel Jim had many dickish moments with Pam, like not telling her about Philly

1398
01:00:14.800 --> 01:00:19.460
and not being very empathetic toward her being alone in Scranton taking care of their family.

1399
01:00:19.460 --> 01:00:25.900
He feels Jim's attitude about Brian is justified and that he was just working hard for their

1400
01:00:25.900 --> 01:00:26.780
family's future.

1401
01:00:27.180 --> 01:00:33.360
The one thing we always admire is how intentionally they chose each other and how they really put in

1402
01:00:33.360 --> 01:00:34.920
the work to improve their relationship.

1403
01:00:35.060 --> 01:00:40.680
And as we are getting married soon and you are both happily married, we would love to hear your

1404
01:00:40.680 --> 01:00:45.240
advice for overcoming rough patches and the secret to a long, happy marriage.

1405
01:00:45.700 --> 01:00:48.960
Well, Brittany, I am very happily married.

1406
01:00:48.960 --> 01:00:54.440
This year is going to be our 14th wedding anniversary and a little over 16 years together.

1407
01:00:54.900 --> 01:01:00.500
But I always like to fall back on some advice that I got from a woman who had been married

1408
01:01:00.500 --> 01:01:03.480
over 40 years, and it always stuck with me.

1409
01:01:03.560 --> 01:01:05.000
So I thought I'd share that.

1410
01:01:05.440 --> 01:01:05.660
Yeah.

1411
01:01:05.900 --> 01:01:08.040
I asked her this same question.

1412
01:01:08.240 --> 01:01:10.400
I was like, 40 years.

1413
01:01:10.880 --> 01:01:12.000
What's the secret?

1414
01:01:12.340 --> 01:01:12.980
How'd you do it?

1415
01:01:12.980 --> 01:01:16.140
Because I knew this couple, and they were this lovely older couple.

1416
01:01:16.480 --> 01:01:21.240
I had met them through church, and they were so happy and wonderful together.

1417
01:01:21.560 --> 01:01:23.400
And she said, well, I'll tell you the secret.

1418
01:01:24.200 --> 01:01:26.020
I've been married to four different men.

1419
01:01:26.460 --> 01:01:26.900
Okay.

1420
01:01:26.980 --> 01:01:27.940
And I was like, what?

1421
01:01:28.640 --> 01:01:31.100
She was like, here's what you can count on.

1422
01:01:31.260 --> 01:01:36.160
You can count on the fact that you all aren't going to change very much and that you're also

1423
01:01:36.160 --> 01:01:37.620
going to change every 10 years.

1424
01:01:37.620 --> 01:01:39.860
And I was like, oh, tell me more.

1425
01:01:40.100 --> 01:01:45.900
And she was like, about every 10 years in our marriage, we had a different season of

1426
01:01:45.900 --> 01:01:46.500
our marriage.

1427
01:01:46.840 --> 01:01:53.080
And I had to look at our season and look at this man, and I had to decide to commit to

1428
01:01:53.080 --> 01:01:53.560
him again.

1429
01:01:53.780 --> 01:01:56.340
And she said, and I've done that about once a decade.

1430
01:01:56.760 --> 01:02:01.560
She said, because, you know, it's so easy in the beginning when it was just us before

1431
01:02:01.560 --> 01:02:02.160
kids.

1432
01:02:02.400 --> 01:02:02.700
Oh, yeah.

1433
01:02:02.880 --> 01:02:04.560
You know, that's a whole season.

1434
01:02:04.560 --> 01:02:10.660
And then, you know, then different jobs would come up, and that stresses a person or changes

1435
01:02:10.660 --> 01:02:11.220
a person.

1436
01:02:11.560 --> 01:02:17.860
And she said, but I'll tell you what, if you pick right from the beginning, and you'll

1437
01:02:17.860 --> 01:02:23.800
know if you're picking right, if you pick right from the beginning, then you'll be able to

1438
01:02:23.800 --> 01:02:27.120
be married to all the versions of the person you're married to.

1439
01:02:27.480 --> 01:02:31.820
She was like, you know, they say marriage is work, and it really is, but it should also be

1440
01:02:31.820 --> 01:02:32.900
the easiest work.

1441
01:02:32.900 --> 01:02:35.120
She said, life is hard.

1442
01:02:35.440 --> 01:02:36.660
Your marriage should be easy.

1443
01:02:37.040 --> 01:02:39.880
And I was like, wow, I really like that.

1444
01:02:40.040 --> 01:02:41.020
I like that, too.

1445
01:02:41.260 --> 01:02:45.500
And this was a woman I met before I had ever married Lee, and then when I started dating

1446
01:02:45.500 --> 01:02:48.400
Lee, I was like, oh, this is what she was talking about.

1447
01:02:48.640 --> 01:02:48.840
Yeah.

1448
01:02:49.800 --> 01:02:55.200
Well, you know, my mom always would say, in the course of a lifetime, romance comes and

1449
01:02:55.200 --> 01:02:55.540
goes.

1450
01:02:55.840 --> 01:02:57.300
So marry your best friend.

1451
01:02:57.500 --> 01:02:57.920
Mm-hmm.

1452
01:02:57.920 --> 01:02:59.840
And Josh is my best friend.

1453
01:03:00.200 --> 01:03:06.680
And then, you know, on a lighthearted note, Brittany, I think a true test of any relationship

1454
01:03:06.680 --> 01:03:10.100
is if you can go to Ikea on a busy day and still have fun.

1455
01:03:12.120 --> 01:03:12.700
That's right.

1456
01:03:12.860 --> 01:03:14.160
Do that before you get married.

1457
01:03:14.480 --> 01:03:17.040
Well, listen, Pam is going to fight for her marriage here.

1458
01:03:17.820 --> 01:03:20.760
She's going to say, I don't think you should go to Philly tonight.

1459
01:03:21.160 --> 01:03:23.960
I think you should stay, and I think we should fight.

1460
01:03:24.460 --> 01:03:25.380
And he agrees.

1461
01:03:25.700 --> 01:03:27.120
It just melts Jim's heart.

1462
01:03:27.120 --> 01:03:27.880
It does.

1463
01:03:28.040 --> 01:03:31.180
He smiles and says, all right, Beasley, put your dukes up.

1464
01:03:31.180 --> 01:03:35.360
A lot of fans noted that Jim calls Pam Beasley at the end of this episode.

1465
01:03:35.660 --> 01:03:38.120
And she loves it when he calls her Beasley.

1466
01:03:38.220 --> 01:03:38.720
That's right.

1467
01:03:38.920 --> 01:03:42.400
And this is, I think, how we know this is going to be okay.

1468
01:03:42.980 --> 01:03:45.240
Well, Erin is going to find Pete in the parking lot.

1469
01:03:45.360 --> 01:03:47.760
A lot happens in the parking lot in this episode.

1470
01:03:48.920 --> 01:03:52.780
She apologizes that she couldn't break up with Andy, and he says, hey, you know what?

1471
01:03:52.800 --> 01:03:54.320
I just want you to be happy.

1472
01:03:54.600 --> 01:03:56.120
And that melts her heart.

1473
01:03:56.380 --> 01:03:57.860
And she gives him a big kiss.

1474
01:03:57.860 --> 01:04:00.440
Well, we got this letter from Catherine P.

1475
01:04:00.600 --> 01:04:03.240
in Redding, Pennsylvania, who said this.

1476
01:04:03.460 --> 01:04:06.920
Just a quick story about why this episode means a lot to me.

1477
01:04:07.260 --> 01:04:10.940
When I was in college, I started talking to this guy that I really liked.

1478
01:04:11.380 --> 01:04:15.380
Suddenly, his ex-girlfriend, who for the record had left him for another guy,

1479
01:04:15.780 --> 01:04:17.780
suddenly resurfaced and wanted him back.

1480
01:04:18.200 --> 01:04:21.340
He came to see me one night after having talked to his ex.

1481
01:04:21.580 --> 01:04:24.780
I told him that while I obviously wanted to be with him,

1482
01:04:25.000 --> 01:04:27.160
what I wanted more was for him to be happy.

1483
01:04:27.160 --> 01:04:33.020
He gave me a big hug and said that his ex had listed all the reasons why she was the right choice,

1484
01:04:33.220 --> 01:04:36.000
but never once told him to do what made him happy.

1485
01:04:36.420 --> 01:04:39.700
It reminded me a lot of how Pete just wanted Erin to be happy.

1486
01:04:40.080 --> 01:04:43.220
Thankfully, Pete and I both got our own happily ever after,

1487
01:04:43.400 --> 01:04:46.940
since I've been with that guy from college for the past 12 years,

1488
01:04:47.000 --> 01:04:48.520
and we've been married for seven.

1489
01:04:48.860 --> 01:04:49.340
Hey.

1490
01:04:49.880 --> 01:04:50.240
Hey.

1491
01:04:51.020 --> 01:04:51.700
Love that.

1492
01:04:52.040 --> 01:04:53.120
Congratulations, Catherine.

1493
01:04:53.260 --> 01:04:54.080
I love that story.

1494
01:04:54.080 --> 01:04:57.620
Erin is now just going to kind of barge right into Andy's office,

1495
01:04:57.620 --> 01:04:59.160
and she's going to say, you know what?

1496
01:04:59.240 --> 01:05:00.080
We're breaking up.

1497
01:05:00.520 --> 01:05:01.980
I thought you were dead.

1498
01:05:02.240 --> 01:05:03.980
You were gone for three months.

1499
01:05:04.340 --> 01:05:07.140
She really just kind of bears her soul to him here in this moment.

1500
01:05:07.200 --> 01:05:08.440
He's kind of speechless.

1501
01:05:08.900 --> 01:05:10.180
And then she leaves.

1502
01:05:10.740 --> 01:05:15.060
Well, we find out that David Wallace was on speakerphone and heard everything.

1503
01:05:15.060 --> 01:05:19.820
And he says, what's this about being gone for three months, Andy?

1504
01:05:20.100 --> 01:05:22.060
And that's how the episode ends.

1505
01:05:22.380 --> 01:05:22.660
Yes.

1506
01:05:24.220 --> 01:05:25.640
Well, there you have it.

